  "cil_liability": false,
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed flat conversion would result in an undue concentration of residential conversions within the locality, given that neighbouring Nos. 24 and 26 Devonshire Road that form part of a consecutive row of five properties on the side of the same side of the street as the application site already comprise two flats each. The development would therefore contribute to the erosion of the existing stock of family housing and would harm the balance and mix of households within the area, to the detriment of the character and function of the established residential neighbourhood. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy DMD5 of the Development Management Document (2014), Policies CP5 and CP30 of the Core Strategy and the NPPF (2026).",
    "The proposed rear dormer and linked roof of the first floor rear extension by virtue of its design, scale and siting when viewed against a backdrop of the character of the proposed roof of the two storey side extension would cumulatively give rise to a contrived, cluttered and bulky form of roof extension that would be detrimental to the character and appearance of the new flats as well as the visual amenities of Devonshire Road.  Additionally, the proposed roof design would introduce a plethora of roof junctions to represent a dominant and incongruous addition and awkward relationship with the parent roofslope, thereby both unbalancing and detracting from the character and symmetry of the prevailing build form within the street scene. The proposals are thereby contrary to Policy D3 of the London Plan (2021), CP30 of the Core Strategy, DMD13 and DMD37 of the Development Management Document and the advice contained within the NPPF (2026).",
    "The proposal would result in a substandard and poor-quality living accommodation by reason of insufficient outlook, sunlight, daylight and privacy implications. The proposed flat 1, bedroom 1, would therefore fail to provide an adequate living environment for prospective occupiers. The proposal is therefore considered contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF, 2026), Policies DMD 3, DMD 6 and DMD 8 of the Development Management Document (2014), Policies CP2, CP4, CP5 and CP30 of the Core Strategy (2010) and policies D3, D4 and D5 of the London Plan (2021) as well as the London Housing SPG.\r\t\r"
  ]
}